  "p29_cor2_thm3": [
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        301.71,
        247.16,
        313.0
      ],
      "text": "when, for example, γ ≤1/5."
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        317.8,
        361.68,
        333.42
      ],
      "text": "Corollary 2. For any a ≥2 and 2a/4 ≤m ≤2a/2,"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        225.8,
        343.6,
        312.88,
        360.84
      ],
      "text": "Q2m(a) ≥2a −O"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        315.2,
        347.34,
        320.68,
        354.99
      ],
      "text": "\u0000"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        320.68,
        343.6,
        371.57,
        359.82
      ],
      "text": "a−1/5 · 2a\u0001"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        373.57,
        349.2,
        376.82,
        359.82
      ],
      "text": "."
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        120.44,
        374.94,
        499.73,
        386.22
      ],
      "text": "We note that already a simpliﬁed version of the strategy from §6 with"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        390.05,
        499.73,
        400.67
      ],
      "text": "the choice of s = 1 and depth 6 would allow us to improve the upper bound"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        404.5,
        499.74,
        415.11
      ],
      "text": "of the method of binary insertions [5] for all suﬃciently large n. However,"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        418.94,
        499.74,
        429.56
      ],
      "text": "to achieve this, we would have to sacriﬁce universality, and carefully select"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        433.39,
        499.73,
        444.0
      ],
      "text": "optimal partitions for any i instead. To get a better bound, we have to choose s"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        447.83,
        146.14,
        458.45
      ],
      "text": "growing."
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        468.26,
        230.15,
        478.87
      ],
      "text": "Theorem 3. For any n,"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        215.17,
        497.28,
        312.73,
        507.9
      ],
      "text": "S(n) = log(n!) + O"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        315.05,
        491.83,
        322.19,
        499.48
      ],
      "text": "\u0010"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        322.19,
        492.14,
        375.05,
        507.9
      ],
      "text": "n log−1/5 n"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        375.06,
        491.83,
        382.2,
        499.48
      ],
      "text": "\u0011"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        384.19,
        497.28,
        387.44,
        507.9
      ],
      "text": "."
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        526.31,
        499.74,
        536.92
      ],
      "text": "Proof. We start as in the method of binary insertions. Divide n inputs into"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        540.75,
        357.73,
        551.37
      ],
      "text": "pairs, order them, and sort the larger elements."
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        367.89,
        540.75,
        499.73,
        551.37
      ],
      "text": "The latter constitute the"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        555.2,
        499.74,
        565.82
      ],
      "text": "principal chain. To do this, we require n/2 + S(n/2) comparisons. We keep"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        569.64,
        413.87,
        582.05
      ],
      "text": "notation αi for the smaller elements in the pairs from Fig. 1."
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        120.44,
        584.09,
        499.74,
        596.5
      ],
      "text": "Next, we proceed with the Ford–Johnson method to insert ﬁrst n0 ="
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        598.54,
        499.73,
        610.95
      ],
      "text": "n/ log n of the elements αi into the principal chain by n + O(n0) compar-"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        603.7,
        499.73,
        623.6
      ],
      "text": "isons. We split the remaining elements into groups of size m ≈√n/ log n."
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        627.43,
        499.73,
        639.85
      ],
      "text": "The group number k contains elements αn0+(k−1)m+1, . . . , αn0+km. Groups are"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        641.87,
        499.73,
        652.49
      ],
      "text": "inserted into the principal chain in turn in ascending order of numbers by the"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        656.32,
        499.74,
        666.94
      ],
      "text": "method of Corollary 2. The last group may contain less than m elements, so"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        670.77,
        330.63,
        681.38
      ],
      "text": "we use trivial binary method to insert them."
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        120.44,
        685.21,
        499.74,
        697.62
      ],
      "text": "The group number t should be inserted into an interval of length Lt ="
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        694.51,
        499.74,
        712.07
      ],
      "text": "2(n0 + tm) −m. By Corollary 2, it holds for some ρt = log Lt + O(log−1/5 n)"
    }
  ],
  "p30_recur": [
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        131.78,
        499.73,
        144.08
      ],
      "text": "that Qm(ρt) ≥Lt. So, the complexity of insertion of the t-th group does not"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        146.22,
        172.0,
        159.3
      ],
      "text": "exceed ρt · m."
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        120.44,
        161.34,
        499.73,
        173.75
      ],
      "text": "Imagine idealistic situation, when any element αk may be inserted into the"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        175.11,
        499.74,
        186.4
      ],
      "text": "principal chain through log(2k −1) comparisons. In this case, to insert all"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        190.23,
        234.92,
        202.64
      ],
      "text": "elements αk we would use"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        156.08,
        226.45,
        171.05,
        237.06
      ],
      "text": "log"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        173.19,
        212.89,
        194.33,
        220.42
      ],
      "text": "⌈n/2⌉"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        176.12,
        222.91,
        191.4,
        230.56
      ],
      "text": "Y"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        176.04,
        243.32,
        191.48,
        250.39
      ],
      "text": "k=1"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        194.33,
        225.78,
        446.54,
        237.06
      ],
      "text": "(2k −1) = log(n!) −log((n/2)!) −n/2 + O(log n)"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        478.93,
        226.45,
        499.74,
        237.06
      ],
      "text": "(45)"
    },
    {
      "bbox": [
        102.88,
        261.06,
